*Arrow syntax provides a more concise way of defining functions.*
***Example***
```js
const sum = function() {
	return a + b;
}
const tax = function tax(a) {
	return 0.05 * a;
}
```

***Arrow syntax equivalent***
```js
const sum = (a,b) => {return a+b}
//or
const sum = (a,b) => return a + b
//or
const sum = (a,b) => a + b

const tax = a => 0.05 * a
```

Let's look at an example with a [[Void]] function
```js
const foo = () => { let a = 3;
				    let b = a;
					alert (a + b);
				  }
```

```js
//Without arrow notation (Literally stab me with a spoon)
setTimeout (function(){alert("hi")}, 2000);

//With that SEXY arrow notation
setTimeout (() => {alert("hi")}, 2000);
```

[[This]] has a different meaning with arrow functions. This isn't an issue but it means it **CANNOT** be used with *constructor functions*, not can you use it on *object literals* 
(only when using [[this]], if you arent using it you can use it in object literals).

[[This]] with display its [[Lexical]] content, which is very helpful when using [[React]].
